    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p><b>"The code is four digits and nothing works."</b> It is eight. The clue on the portrait only constrains the two ends — starts with 3, ends with 8 — and the keypad has no display, so there is nothing in the room that tells you the length. If you are typing four digits and the safe just sits there, that is expected: keep going.</p>
    <p><b>"I typed 3468 and it did not open."</b> 3468 is the door plate plus the toilet-roll digit, and it looks like the whole answer. It is not — it skips the 1 and 7 on the paper and both middle keys. The full order is 346 1705 8.</p>
    <p><b>"I cannot find 17 anywhere."</b> The Piece of paper is an inventory item, not a wall clue. If your bag is empty, you have not picked up the hexagonal scrap yet; open the inventory and select it to see the two characters enlarged. If the bag is empty and the paper is not in the room, you have not opened the cabinet.</p>
    <p><b>"The door wants a code."</b> It does not. There is no keypad on the door, no dial, no escutcheon keypad — just a lever handle. 346 is painted there as a room number and doubles as the seed for the safe. The door opens with the <b>Key</b> that is already in your bag by 0:55, and with nothing else.</p>
    <p><b>"The toilet and the floor must do something."</b> Neither does. The bowl is empty and the patterned floor is decoration. The only thing on that side of the room that matters is the cardboard roll.</p>
    <p><b>"A and B must be enter and clear."</b> Probably, but the recording never presses either of them: the eight digits go in and the safe releases on its own. If your safe needs a confirmation key, try A — that is the most common layout — but we cannot confirm it from this footage.</p>

    <h2>Where Guest Toilet sits in Chapter 3<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Chapter 3 is <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and Guest Toilet is room <b>4 of 15</b>, right after Study Room. It is the smallest room in the chapter's opening run and the first one where the puzzle chain is entirely made of printed digits rather than mechanisms: no sliding panels, no colour sequences, no wiring. Three props, one clue sentence, one keypad.</p>
    <p>It also breaks the pattern the first three rooms set, where the code is a clean four or five digits. Here the keypad takes eight, and the only way to know that is to count the keys being pressed. If you are working through the chapter in order, that is the habit worth carrying forward: on a safe with no display, count the presses rather than assuming a length.</p>

